Get the number of structural nonzeros of one input, or of all inputs.

Without argument, the sum over all inputs. The nonzero count is what
numerical evaluation reads for the input; it is smaller than numel_in for
sparse inputs.



::

  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [SX.sym(\"x\", 2), SX.sym(\"D\", Sparsity.diag(3))], [SX(1)])
  >>> f.nnz_in(0), f.nnz_in(1), f.nnz_in()
  (2, 3, 5)
  >>> f.numel_in(0), f.numel_in(1), f.numel_in()
  (2, 9, 11)
  





