%feature("docstring") casadi::Function::bspline "

Create a function evaluating a B-spline with known coefficients.

The result maps a point of dimension len(knots) (one column vector input) to
the m-dimensional spline value. Coefficients are stored flat in ``coeffs``
with the value dimension m fastest, then the first spline dimension, etc.;
the number of coefficients per dimension is len(knots[d]) - degree[d] - 1. A
mismatch between the lengths of ``knots`` and ``degree`` raises an error.
For data-driven splines or general interpolation see interpolant.



Parameters:
    name: Name of the result
    knots: Knot vector per dimension, non-decreasing, with repeated end knots
    coeffs: Flat coefficient list
    degree: Polynomial degree per dimension
    m: Dimension of the spline value
    opts: \"lookup_mode\" (per dimension: \"linear\", \"exact\", \"binary\") and Function options



::

  >>> f = Function.bspline(\"f\", [[0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 2]], [0, 1, 2, 3], [2])
  >>> f
  Function(f:(i0)->(o0) MXFunction)
  >>> f(0), f(0.5), f(2)
  (DM(0), DM(0.875), DM(3))
  >>> g = Function.bspline(\"g\", [[0, 0, 1, 1], [0, 0, 1, 1]], [0, 1, 2, 3], [1, 1])
  >>> g([0.5, 0.5])
  DM(1.5)
  >>> try:
  ...   Function.bspline(\"f\", [[0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 2]], [0, 1, 2, 3], [2, 2])
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"must match knot list length\" in str(e))
  True
  





See also: interpolant, MX::bspline

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1wo

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L2252
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L943-L960
";

%feature("docstring") casadi::Function::if_else "

Create a function that evaluates ``f_true`` or ``f_false`` depending on.

a condition input

The result takes the condition as first input followed by the inputs of the
branches; a nonzero condition (truncated to integer) selects ``f_true``,
zero selects ``f_false``. Only the selected branch is evaluated numerically.
The signature is taken from ``f_false`` (see conditional).



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\")
  >>> f = Function.if_else(\"f\", Function(\"t\", [x], [x**2]), Function(\"e\", [x], [x]))
  >>> f
  Function(f:(i0,i1)->(o0) Switch)
  >>> f(1, 3), f(0, 3), f(5, 3)
  (DM(9), DM(3), DM(9))
  





See also: conditional

