%feature("docstring") casadi::Function::which_depends "

Find which entries of an input the outputs depend on, linearly or not.

Returns one boolean per nonzero of input ``s_in:`` true when at least one of
the outputs ``s_out`` depends on that entry (``order`` 1) or depends on it
nonlinearly (``order`` 2). With ``tr`` true the relation is flipped: one
boolean per nonzero of the vertically concatenated outputs, true when it
depends on any entry of ``s_in``. The analysis is structural (it follows the
graph, not the values).



Parameters:
    s_in: Name of one input
    s_out: Names of the outputs to inspect
    order: 1 (any dependence) or 2 (nonlinear dependence); other values raise
    tr: Transpose the relation, see above



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y[0], y[1]**2 + x, 3], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])
  >>> f.which_depends(\"y\", [\"a\", \"b\"])
  [True, True]
  >>> f.which_depends(\"y\", [\"a\", \"b\"], 2)
  [False, True]
  >>> f.which_depends(\"y\", [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], 1, True)
  [True, True, False]
  >>> f.which_depends(\"x\", [\"a\"], 2)
  [False]
  





See also: factory, jac_sparsity

