// File: classcasadi_1_1FunctionBuffer.xml
%feature("docstring") casadi::FunctionBuffer "

Evaluate a Function repeatedly with minimal overhead.

A FunctionBuffer holds the work vectors and a memory object of a Function
and evaluates it directly on caller-owned contiguous double buffers,
skipping argument conversion and allocation. Bind each input with set_arg
and each output with set_res (nonzeros in column-major order, see
nz_from_in), then evaluate; the results appear in the bound output buffers
and ret() gives the return code (0 on success). The buffers must stay alive
and unchanged in size while the FunctionBuffer is used.

In Python, Function.buffer() returns a pair (FunctionBuffer, callable): the
callable runs one evaluation; bind numpy arrays with memoryview.



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\"])
  >>> fb, evaluate = f.buffer()
  >>> a = np.array([2.0]); b = np.array([3.0, 4.0]); r = np.zeros(2)
  >>> fb.set_arg(0, memoryview(a)); fb.set_arg(1, memoryview(b))
  >>> fb.set_res(0, memoryview(r))
  >>> evaluate()
  >>> r, fb.ret()
  (array([6., 8.]), 0)
  >>> a[0] = 5; evaluate(); print(r)
  [15. 20.]
  >>> try:
  ...   fb.set_arg(1, memoryview(a))
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"Buffer is not large enough\" in str(e))
  True
  



::

  \\\\htmlonly <div class='doctest doctest-matlab'> \\\\endhtmlonly \\\\verbatim
  >> x = SX.sym('x');
  >> isempty(fieldnames(Function('f', {x}, {x}).info()))
  1
  



See also: Function::nz_from_in, Function::checkout, Function::sz_w

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1y9

";

