%feature("docstring") casadi::GenericExpressionCommon::mod "

Remainder after division: (x,y) -> fmod(x,y)

This Function follows the convention of
https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/numeric/math/fmod:  the result has the sign
of ``x`` and magnitude below |y|.

This is equivalent to Python's numpy.fmod and Matlab's rem. It differs from
the Python operator x % y on these objects, which follows the Python
convention x - y*floor(x/y) (result with the sign of ``y``). Elementwise,
shape rules as for plus; structural zeros of ``x`` stay zero. A zero divisor
gives NaN.



::

  >>> fmod(DM([5, 5, -5, -5]), DM([3, -3, 3, -3]))
  DM([2, 2, -2, -2])
  >>> DM([5, 5, -5, -5]) % DM([3, -3, 3, -3])
  DM([2, -1, 1, -2])
  >>> fmod(SX.sym(\"x\"), 2), SX.sym(\"x\") % 2
  (SX(fmod(x,2)), SX((x-(2.*floor((x/2))))))
  





See also: remainder, floor  Extra doc:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_pq



