/* Construct symbolic primitives */

/* The \"sym\" function is intended to work in a similar way as \"sym\" used
in the Symbolic Toolbox for Matlab but instead creating a CasADi symbolic
primitive.

*/ %feature("docstring") casadi::MatrixCommon "

Sparse matrix class. SX and DM are specializations.

General sparse matrix class that is designed with the idea that \"everything
is a matrix\", that is, also scalars and vectors. This philosophy makes it
easy to use and to interface in particularly with Python and Matlab/Octave.
Index starts with 0. Index vec happens as follows: (rr, cc) -> k =
rr+cc*size1() Vectors are column vectors. The storage format is Compressed
Column Storage (CCS), similar to that used for sparse matrices in Matlab,
but unlike this format, we do allow for elements to be structurally non-zero
but numerically zero. Matrix<Scalar> is polymorphic with a
std::vector<Scalar> that contain all non-identical-zero elements. The
sparsity can be accessed with Sparsity& sparsity() SX holds symbolic scalar
entries (SXElem), DM holds doubles. A structural zero prints as 00, a
numerical zero as 0. The structural nonzeros are available as a flat column-
major vector with nonzeros().



::

  >>> A = DM([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
  >>> A.sparsity().dim(), A.nonzeros()
  ('2x2', [1.0, 3.0, 2.0, 4.0])
  >>> S = DM(Sparsity.diag(2))
  >>> S
  DM(
  [[1, 00],
   [00, 1]])
  >>> S.nnz(), S.numel()
  (2, 4)
  





Authors: Joel Andersson

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_18e

";

%feature("docstring") casadi::Matrix< double >::get "

Get a submatrix by row and column indices.

Backs the Python expression x[i, j]. Each of ``rr`` and ``cc`` is a scalar,
a Slice or a list/vector of indices (any order, repetitions allowed,
negative counts from the end). The result has len(rr) rows and len(cc)
columns and keeps the sparsity of the selected entries. Two scalar indices
give a 1-by-1 matrix (structurally empty when the entry is a structural
zero). Out-of-range indices raise an error.



Parameters:
    ind1: True for one-based indices (MATLAB); Python always passes false
    rr: Row index, Slice or list/matrix of row indices
    cc: Column index, Slice or list/matrix of column indices



::

  >>> A = DM([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])
  >>> A[0, 1], A[1, -1]
  (DM(2), DM(6))
  >>> A[:, 1]
  DM([2, 5])
  >>> A[[1, 0], [0, 2]]
  DM(
  [[4, 6],
   [1, 3]])
  >>> A[1:1, :].dim()
  '0x3'
  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\", 2, 2)
  >>> x[0, :]
  SX([[x_0, x_2]])
  >>> DM(Sparsity.diag(2))[1, 0]
  DM(00)
  >>> try: A[2, 0]
  ... except Exception as e: print(\"error\")
  error
  





