/* Construct symbolic primitives */

/* The \"sym\" function is intended to work in a similar way as \"sym\" used
in the Symbolic Toolbox for Matlab but instead creating a CasADi symbolic
primitive.

*/ %feature("docstring") casadi::MX "

MX - Matrix expression.

The MX class is used to build up trees made up from MXNodes. It is a more
general graph representation than the scalar expression, SX, and much less
efficient for small objects. On the other hand, the class allows much more
general operations than does SX, in particular matrix valued operations and
calls to arbitrary differentiable functions.

The MX class is designed to have identical syntax with the Matrix<> template
class, and uses DM (i.e. Matrix<double>) as its internal representation of
the values at a node. By keeping the syntaxes identical, it is possible to
switch from one class to the other, as well as inlining MX functions to
SXElem functions.

Note that an operation is always \"lazy\", making a matrix multiplication
will create a matrix multiplication node, not perform the actual
multiplication.

Each node is a matrix-valued operation (symbolic primitive, constant, binary
operation, concatenation, matrix product, call to a Function, ...), so an
expression such as A +1 is a small number of nodes regardless of the matrix
sizes. The graph is immutable: every operation creates new nodes. Symbolic
primitives are created with MX.sym; MX(...) creates constants.



::

  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\", 2)
  >>> A = MX.sym(\"A\", 2, 2)
  >>> e = A @ x + 1
  >>> e
  MX((ones(2x1)+mac(A,x,zeros(2x1))))
  >>> e.shape, e.n_dep(), e.is_symbolic()
  ((2, 1), 2, False)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [A, x], [e])
  >>> f(DM([[1, 2], [3, 4]]), DM([1, 1]))
  DM([4, 8])
  





See also: SX, DM, Function



Authors: Joel Andersson

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_py

";

%feature("docstring") casadi::MX::binary "

Create an elementwise binary or unary node from an operation code.

Low-level constructors behind the operators: MX.binary(OP_ADD, x, y) is x +
y. Operands must have the same shape, or one of them is a scalar, or one is
a horizontal repetition of the other (which is repmat-ed). The usual
simplifications are applied. An invalid operation code is not checked.



Parameters:
    op: One of the OP_* constants
    unique_x, unique_y: Internal hints for in-place simplification; leave false



::

  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\")
  >>> y = MX.sym(\"y\")
  >>> MX.binary(OP_ADD, x, y), MX.unary(OP_SIN, x)
  (MX((x+y)), MX(sin(x)))
  >>> MX.binary(OP_ADD, MX.sym(\"V\", 2, 4), MX.sym(\"W\", 2, 2))
  MX((V+repmat(W, 2)))
  >>> try:
  ...   MX.binary(OP_ADD, MX.sym(\"v\", 2), MX.sym(\"w\", 3))
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Dimension mismatch\" in str(e))
  True
  





See also: op

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_r1

