%feature("docstring") casadi::Opti::solver "

Select the numerical solver and its options.

Must be called before solve(). The solver is (re)constructed lazily at the
next solve() when this method or the problem structure changed.



Parameters:
    solver: Name of an nlpsol plugin (\"ipopt\", \"sqpmethod\", \"bonmin\",...) or, for a \"conic\" Opti, of a qpsol plugin (\"qrqp\", \"qpoases\", \"osqp\",...)
    plugin_options: Dict passed as-is to nlpsol/qpsol: CasADi-level options such as \"print_time\", \"expand\", \"detect_simple_bounds\", \"error_on_fail\", \"iteration_callback\", and plugin options that are registered as CasADi options (e.g. \"qpsol\" and \"print_iteration\" of sqpmethod, \"print_iter\" of qrqp)
    solver_options: Dict of the back-end's native options; it is nested under the key ``solver``, so {\"max_iter\": 50} here equals {\"ipopt\": {\"max_iter\": 50}} in ``plugin_options``. Opti also sets the nlpsol options \"equality\" and \"discrete\" unless given explicitly.

No stability is guaranteed for option names; they belong to the plugins.



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize((x-2)**2)
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.solve()
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"You must call 'solver' on the Opti stack\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> round(opti.solve().value(x), 4)
  2.0
  >>> opti.solver(\"sqpmethod\", {\"print_time\": False, \"print_header\": False,
  ...   \"print_iteration\": False, \"print_status\": False, \"qpsol\": \"qrqp\",
  ...   \"qpsol_options\": {\"print_iter\": False, \"print_header\": False, \"print_info\": False}})
  >>> round(opti.solve().value(x), 4)
  2.0
  





See also: nlpsol, qpsol, solve

