%feature("docstring") casadi::OptiAdvanced::solver "

Select the numerical solver and its options.

Must be called before solve(). The solver is (re)constructed lazily at the
next solve() when this method or the problem structure changed.



Parameters:
    solver: Name of an nlpsol plugin (\"ipopt\", \"sqpmethod\", \"bonmin\",...) or, for a \"conic\" Opti, of a qpsol plugin (\"qrqp\", \"qpoases\", \"osqp\",...)
    plugin_options: Dict passed as-is to nlpsol/qpsol: CasADi-level options such as \"print_time\", \"expand\", \"detect_simple_bounds\", \"error_on_fail\", \"iteration_callback\", and plugin options that are registered as CasADi options (e.g. \"qpsol\" and \"print_iteration\" of sqpmethod, \"print_iter\" of qrqp)
    solver_options: Dict of the back-end's native options; it is nested under the key ``solver``, so {\"max_iter\": 50} here equals {\"ipopt\": {\"max_iter\": 50}} in ``plugin_options``. Opti also sets the nlpsol options \"equality\" and \"discrete\" unless given explicitly.

No stability is guaranteed for option names; they belong to the plugins.



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize((x-2)**2)
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.solve()
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"You must call 'solver' on the Opti stack\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> round(opti.solve().value(x), 4)
  2.0
  >>> opti.solver(\"sqpmethod\", {\"print_time\": False, \"print_header\": False,
  ...   \"print_iteration\": False, \"print_status\": False, \"qpsol\": \"qrqp\",
  ...   \"qpsol_options\": {\"print_iter\": False, \"print_header\": False, \"print_info\": False}})
  >>> round(opti.solve().value(x), 4)
  2.0
  





See also: nlpsol, qpsol, solve

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1c

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L566
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L155-L163
";

%feature("docstring") casadi::OptiAdvanced::set_domain "

Set the domain of a decision variable.

Marks all scalars of ``x`` as integer or real (default). The information is
passed to the solver as the nlpsol/qpsol option \"discrete\"; a solver
without integer support raises an error at solve().



Parameters:
    x: A decision variable (pure symbol, not an expression)
    domain: \"real\" or \"integer\"; anything else raises an error



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.set_domain(x, \"integer\")
  >>> opti.minimize((x-1.4)**2)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.solve()
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  Discrete variables require a solver with integer support
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.set_domain(x, \"binary\")
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Unknown domain 'binary'. Known values are 'real', 'integer'.\" in str(e))
  True
  



Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_27t

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L736
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L189-L195
";

%feature("docstring") casadi::OptiAdvanced::set_linear_scale "

Set the linear scaling of a decision variable.

The solver works with (x-offset)/scale instead of x; all user-facing values
(value(), initial guesses, duals) stay in the original units. Useful when
variables differ by orders of magnitude.



Parameters:
    x: Decision variable or linear mapping of variables
    scale: Scalar or matrix of the shape of ``x`` (default 1)
    offset: Scalar or matrix of the shape of ``x`` (default 0)



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> T = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.set_linear_scale(T, 20, 273.15)
  >>> opti.minimize((T-300)**2)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> round(sol.value(T), 3), opti.x_linear_scale, opti.x_linear_scale_offset
  (300.0, DM(20), DM(273.15))
  >>> round(float(opti.debug.arg()[\"x0\"]), 4)
  -13.6575
  





See also: x_linear_scale, x_linear_scale_offset, scale_helper

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2bs

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L777
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L197-L203
";

%feature("docstring") casadi::OptiAdvanced::solve "

Solve the problem.

Builds (or reuses) the solver, evaluates the bounds for the current
parameter values, runs the solver from the current initial guess and returns
an OptiSol snapshot. Raises an error when the solver does not report success
(including iteration or time limits, see solve_limited()); the latest
iterate then remains available through opti.debug.value() and opti.value().
Calling solve() again without changing anything repeats the same solve from
the same initial guess.



Returns:
    OptiSol copy of the problem and its solution



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize((x-1)**6)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False},
  ...             {\"print_level\": 0, \"max_iter\": 2, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.solve()
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Solver failed\" in str(e), \"'Maximum_Iterations_Exceeded'\" in str(e))
  True True
  >>> round(opti.debug.value(x), 2), opti.return_status()
