/* Size and element counting */ %feature("docstring") casadi::Sparsity "

General sparsity class.

The storage format is a compressed column storage (CCS) format. In this
format, the structural non-zero elements are stored in column-major order,
starting from the upper left corner of the matrix and ending in the lower
right corner.

In addition to the dimension (size1(), size2()), (i.e. the number of rows
and the number of columns respectively), there are also two vectors of
integers:



1. \"colind\" [length size2() +1], which contains the index to the first non-zero element on or after the corresponding column. All the non-zero elements of a particular column i are thus the elements with index el that fulfills: colind[i] <= el < colind[i+1].
2. \"row\" [same length as the number of non-zero elements, nnz()] The rows for each of the structural non-zeros.



Note that with this format, it is cheap to loop over all the non-zero
elements of a particular column, at constant time per element, but expensive
to jump to access a location (i, j).

If the matrix is dense, i.e. length(row) == size1()*size2(), the format
reduces to standard dense column major format, which allows access to an
arbitrary element in constant time.

Since the object is reference counted (it inherits from SharedObject),
several matrices are allowed to share the same sparsity pattern.

The implementations of methods marked as such in this class has been taken
from the CSparse package and modified to fit CasADi data structures and
separation of sparsity pattern calculation and numerical evaluation. These
functions are Copyright(c) Timothy A. Davis, 2006-2009 and licensed as a
derivative work under the GNU LGPL

Every DM, SX and MX carries a pattern, obtained with sparsity(), and a
symbolic or numeric matrix can be created from one, e.g. SX.sym(\"x\", sp)
or DM(sp, 1). Entries outside the pattern are structural zeros: they are
never stored and never computed with. The row indices within a column are
strictly increasing.

Patterns are immutable and cached: constructing a pattern that already
exists returns a reference to the cached object, so equal patterns are cheap
to compare. The in-place methods (resize, enlarge, erase, append, add_nz,
...) rebind the object to a new pattern rather than modify the shared one.
Use is_equal or the == operator to compare patterns; object identity is not
meaningful in the target languages.

A default-constructed Sparsity is a null object (is_null() is true): it
carries no pattern and most methods raise an error on it. It is used as \"no
pattern\" sentinel, e.g. as the result of a coloring that exceeds its
cutoff. Empty patterns are allowed: a 0-by-n or n-by-0 pattern has no
nonzeros, is dense, and is_empty() is true for it.



::

     >>> sp = Sparsity.triplet(3, 4, [0, 2, 1, 0], [0, 0, 2, 3])
     >>> DM(sp, 1)
     DM(
     [[1, 00, 00, 1],
      [00, 00, 1, 00],
      [1, 00, 00, 00]])
     >>> sp
     Sparsity(3x4,4nz)
     >>> sp.size1(), sp.size2(), sp.nnz(), sp.numel()
     (3, 4, 4, 12)
     >>> sp.colind(), sp.row()
     ([0, 2, 2, 3, 4], [0, 2, 1, 0])
     >>> sp == Sparsity.triplet(3, 4, [0, 2, 1, 0], [0, 0, 2, 3])
     True
     >>> SX.sym(\"x\", sp)
     SX(
     [[x_0, 00, 00, x_3],
      [00, 00, x_2, 00],
      [x_1, 00, 00, 00]])
     >>> Sparsity.dense(0, 3), Sparsity().is_null()
     (Sparsity(0x3), True)





See also: Matrix



Authors: Joel Andersson

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_b9

";

%feature("docstring") casadi::Sparsity::is_equal "

Check whether two sparsity patterns are identical.

True when the dimensions and the nonzero positions coincide. This is the
comparison behind the == operator. Shared (cached) patterns are detected in
constant time; otherwise colind and row are compared. The second overload
compares against a pattern given in compressed column storage vectors.



::

  >>> a = Sparsity.diag(2)
  >>> a.is_equal(Sparsity.diag(2)), a == Sparsity.diag(2), a != Sparsity.dense(2, 2)
  (True, True, True)
  >>> a.is_equal(2, 2, [0, 1, 2], [0, 1])
  True
  >>> Sparsity.dense(2, 2) == Sparsity.dense(2, 3)
  False
  >>> Sparsity(2, 2) == Sparsity.dense(2, 2)
  False
  





See also: is_subset, is_transpose, is_reshape, hash

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2no

