%feature("docstring") casadi::SXElem::mod "

Remainder after division: (x,y) -> fmod(x,y)

This Function follows the convention of
https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/numeric/math/fmod:  the result has the sign
of ``x`` and magnitude below |y|.

This is equivalent to Python's numpy.fmod and Matlab's rem. It differs from
the Python operator x % y on these objects, which follows the Python
convention x - y*floor(x/y) (result with the sign of ``y``). Elementwise,
shape rules as for plus; structural zeros of ``x`` stay zero. A zero divisor
gives NaN.



::

  >>> fmod(DM([5, 5, -5, -5]), DM([3, -3, 3, -3]))
  DM([2, 2, -2, -2])
  >>> DM([5, 5, -5, -5]) % DM([3, -3, 3, -3])
  DM([2, -1, 1, -2])
  >>> fmod(SX.sym(\"x\"), 2), SX.sym(\"x\") % 2
  (SX(fmod(x,2)), SX((x-(2.*floor((x/2))))))
  





See also: remainder, floor  Extra doc:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_pq



Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/generic_expression.hpp#L1463
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/generic_expression.hpp#L1463-L1465
";

%feature("docstring") casadi::SXElem::remainder "

Remainder after division: (x,y) -> remainder(x,y)

This Function follows the convention of
https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/numeric/math/remainder:  x - n*y with n the
integer nearest to x/y (ties to even), so the result lies in [-|y|/2,
|y|/2].

Notably:

- remainder(5,3) -> -1
- remainder(5,-3) -> -1
- remainder(-5,3) -> 1
- remainder(-5,-3) -> 1



This is equivalent to Python's math.remainder. There is no equivalence in
Matlab. Elementwise, shape rules as for plus; structural zeros of ``x`` stay
zero.



::

  >>> remainder(DM([5, 5, -5, -5]), DM([3, -3, 3, -3]))
  DM([-1, -1, 1, 1])
  >>> remainder(DM([2.5, 3.5]), 2)
  DM([0.5, -0.5])
  >>> remainder(MX.sym(\"x\"), 2)
  MX(remainder(x,2))
  





See also: fmod  Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_24x



