";

%feature("docstring") casadi::SXElem::fmin "

Smallest of two values: (x,y) -> min(x,y)

Elementwise minimum; shape rules as for plus. Follows C fmin: when one
operand is NaN the other is returned, NaN results only when both are NaN.
The derivative is 1 with respect to the smaller operand (split evenly on
ties). The sparsity pattern is kept where both operands are structurally
zero; fmin with a negative scalar densifies. For the minimum over the
entries of one matrix use mmin.



::

  >>> fmin(DM([1, 5]), DM([3, 2]))
  DM([1, 2])
  >>> fmin(DM([1, float(\"nan\")]), DM([float(\"nan\"), 2]))
  DM([1, 2])
  >>> fmin(DM.eye(2), 1)
  DM(
  [[1, 00],
   [00, 1]])
  >>> fmin(SX.sym(\"x\"), SX.sym(\"y\"))
  SX(fmin(x,y))
  





See also: fmax, mmin

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_pt

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/generic_expression.hpp#L1612
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/generic_expression.hpp#L1612-L1614
";

